RKF45 (Runge–Kutta–Fehlberg) adapts the step size \(h\) at every step, instead of the fixed \(h=b/n\) used in Explicit Methods. Six slope evaluations \(k_1,\dots,k_6\), each of the general Runge-Kutta form
\[
k_i = f\!\left(t_n + c_i h,\ y_n + h\sum_{j&lt;i} a_{ij}k_j\right),
\]
combine into two embedded estimates of \(y_{n+1}\) of different order, using two different weight sets \(b_i\), \(b_i^*\) on the same six \(k_i\):
\[
y_{n+1}^{(4)} = y_n + h\sum_{i=1}^{6} b_i k_i \qquad\text{(4th order)}, \qquad
y_{n+1}^{(5)} = y_n + h\sum_{i=1}^{6} b_i^{*} k_i \qquad\text{(5th order)}.
\]
If \(\left|y_{n+1}^{(5)} - y_{n+1}^{(4)}\right|\) exceeds a target tolerance \(\varepsilon\), the step is rejected and retried with a smaller \(h\); otherwise it’s accepted (using \(y_{n+1}^{(5)}\)) and \(h\) grows for the next step.

